From da2548ed746efa2b81fed2d5c299069807a98bae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 11:00:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] expand: a block's `macro_rules!` is in scope for the block's
 items after it

Items written in a block go to its anon module, and Expand ran over all of
them before walking the block's statements, which is where a block-level
`macro_rules!` is registered. wasmparser 0.244.0 (tree-sitter-cli 0.27.1),
validator/core.rs `check_const_expr`:

    macro_rules! define_visit_operator { .. }
    ..
    impl<'a> VisitOperator<'a> for VisitConstOperator<'a> {
        crate::for_each_visit_operator!(define_visit_operator);
    }

`for_each_visit_operator!` expands to `define_visit_operator! { .. }`, which
was unknown at that point, and Expand_Impl asserted instead of deferring:

    MACRO<::"wasmparser-0_244_0_H3c064"::crate::for_each_visit_operator> BUG: ASSERT FAIL: src/expand/mod.cpp:1865:ttl: TODO: Unexpanded macro?

When an outer macro has the same name, the block's items silently used that
one instead: rustc's textual scope gives the items after a block's
definition the block's macro, and the ones before it the outer one.

The parser now records on a block's `macro_rules!` how many of the block's
items came before it (ExprNode_Macro::m_block_items_before). The block
visitor expands the items in runs: up to the first definition before the
statement walk, up to the next definition each time the walk has registered
one, and the rest (with the items the walk adds) after the walk. A run stops
at an item address taken before anything expands, as item macros expand in
place. Expand_Mod takes that stop item. Blocks without a `macro_rules!` in
their source are expanded as before.

Impl and trait item macros that are still unknown now wait for the next
pass, like module and extern block items (the `else` branches were already
there). That covers a definition that a statement macro expands to, and
an item macro that is never defined is now an "Unknown macro" error, not a BUG.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
